and during  the 1930s and 40s as i'm sure many of  you know jews were hunted by nazis  during this time and over 6 million of  them were killed in death camps simply  because of the way that they were born  understanding this is of course crucial  to understanding why eric hates humanity  so much as he has seen firsthand how  people's prejudices can lead to  extermination i've been at the mercy of  men just following orders  never again in the x-men franchise we  see as someone's powers manifest during  times of stress and their adolescence  and eric being separated from his family  kickstarter's ability to manipulate  metal i remember seeing this scene in  the cinema for the first time all the  way back in 2000 and it blew me away as  i knew that cinema would be changed from  that point onwards superhero movies up  until len hadn't really tackled any  truly dark subject matter yet here we  were faced with one of the worst  atrocities ever committed birthing one  of the most powerful supervillains ever  now where things shifted was that we  were introduced to a brand new character  in kevin bacon sebastian shaw if you've  studied history then you'll know that  these death camps were filled with  scientists carrying out human  experiments in order to quote unquote  improve humanity shaw very much  represents these scientists and he makes  the perfect villain as we know from real  life exactly how bad that they were  hitler believed in the aryan superman a  race of blonde head and blue-eyed people  that he thought descended from the  nordic gods what's worrying about this  scene is that we know because of his  abilities that eric will be studied and  this is confirmed when we see shaw  telling him to move a coin eric is  unable to do this and this sure has his  mother brought in who he then executes  in order to trigger his abilities it's  the callousness here that lets you know  how little shaw thinks of humanity and  also sets up a quest for revenge within  eric that drives him throughout the  entire movie interestingly the five  reichsmark coin was made of silver which  has a weaker magnetic structure than  most medals so even if eric was slightly  more advanced with his abilities he  would struggle moving this even when  unleashing his powers in the room the  coin doesn't shift and sure places it in  his hand which starts off the quest to  avenge his mother from here we go to the  title sequence before joining him as an  adult still hunting for the man who  destroyed his family the scene here  beautifully foreshadows the end of the  film as eric flies the coin directly  into a picture of shaw's forehead which  sets up exactly how he kills him at the  end of the film  now the movie follows eric as he teams  up with xavier and the x-men in order to  stop shaw's starting nuclear war between  russia and america we experience the  cuban missile crisis firsthand and eric  rushes to stop the villain who up until  this point has been blocking out  professor x by using a helmet that stops  telepaths inside the reactor of his  submarine eric encounters his enemy who  apologizes for what happened in the  camps when he was a child however he  states that he did this in order to make  him stronger and we very much watch as  his callousness is reflected in eric the  reactor itself is sealed off and thus  xavier can't get through to his mind but  after using himself as bait eric  destroys the room and brings forth pipes  and beams whilst shaw goes off on his  villain monologue he pins eric against  the wall and implants the idea on him  that they are the future of the human  race we are the future of the human race  it seems like eric is going to join him  but this is a trap and he pulls the  character's helmet off and thus charles  is able to enter his mind and hold him  steady now what happens next is  breathtaking and eric steps forward  takes the helmet for himself and at this  point he becomes the villain we know and  love staring shaw directly in the face  he says  if you're in there  i'd like you to know that i agree with  every word you said it's chilling as we  know that magneto genuinely believes  that shaw is right and that humanity  must be extinguished in order for the  mutants to rise up however there's just  one problem shaw killed his mother and  he can't let that slide perfectly  mirroring the scene from earlier in the  movie eric holds up the reichsmark and  says that he's going to count to three  and then move the coin just in the same  way that he was helpless as a child shaw  is in the same position unable to do  anything and simply stuck there watching  his death play out eric has the chance  to be the better man but because he's so  fixated on revenge he doesn't care  anymore i would rather have his mother's  death avenge than be part of the new  family that we watched him create  throughout the movie it gives me chills  every time i watch this and eric slowly  moves the coin through the air towards  shaw's head who due to his abilities  immediately absorbs it however because  he's not in control of the situation  he's unable to manipulate the incoming  object and thus it rips directly through  his brain now the person who also has to  feel the weight of this is of course  charles who's stuck in an impossible  situation if he let sure go he will of  course succeed and destroy all of  humanity however if he holds him in  place he'll be murdered and charles will  partly be responsible for this xavier is  a person who would rather always save a  life than cost it and this has been seen  throughout the franchise hell even in  days of future past we watched as he had  wolverine sent back in time to save  mystique when the easier option would  have been to basically sarah conoho he's  someone who will try and protect life no  matter what but here his hands are  completely tied and all that he can do  is watch on as eric rips through the  head of his nemesis the direction here  is outstanding too and just as we pan  across with the coin tearing through  shore's head we watch as the same camera  movement happens with charles who simply  stands there screaming as he sees it all  play out it's one of the best death  scenes in a comic book movie ever and it  subtly hints to us how both charles and  eric will be on opposite sides of the  fight from this point onwards charles is  someone who can of course control  people's minds but here he's unable to  convince eric to do the right thing and  it shows just how powerless that he  really is it really speaks to how even  with the best intentions that it's  impossible to change some things and it  really hits home why we are all likely  doomed to travel down a darker path if  we live with hate in our hearts they are  truly broken apart because of this and  whereas charles would rather save his  enemies aka the humans eric is happy to  kill them in order to achieve his goals  it's symbolic on a number of levels and  just as we watch shaw get taken down we  see someone with the same objectives  same helmet and almost the same team  step into his place the worst thing is  that we know magneto was born out of  pain and that from this point onwards  the quest for revenge will never stop  for him he emerges from the submarine a  new man and he decides to launch the  missiles in order to allow humanity to  destroy itself charles tries to convince  him that the men on the boat are just  following orders and we know from our  own history that this is what many of  those complicit in the holocaust gave us  an excuse for why they did the things  they did it truly is the moment that one  of the world's best supervillains is  born and every time i watch this scene i  just get chills whether it's magneto  accepting that he agrees with shaw  moving the coin or knowing that charles  is completely helpless it has it all we  can truly see from the film exactly why  magneto came to be this way and it also  explains why he'll travel on the path  that he does in the future what's  heartbreaking is that after charles is  shot eric holds him in his arms and  thinks that they're still brothers he  says that they want the same things but  xavier shoots down this idea and says  that they don't it really is a great  scene that separates the two and though  i was going to do the bar moment from  earlier in the film i think this just  edges it out in terms of bringing the  arc full circle and also giving birth to  a man who ended up becoming a monster  whilst trying to destroy them it ends  the film in an incredible way and for me  is completely perfect  obviously i'd love to hear your thoughts  on the scene so make sure you comment  below and let me know we are running a  competition right now and giving away  three copies of zack snyder's dc trilogy  on the 30th of september all you have to  do to be on the chance of winning is  like the video make sure you subscribe  with notifications on and drop a comment  below with your thoughts on the scene we  picked the comments at random at the end  of the month and the winners of the last  one are on screen right now say that to  you then message me on twitter  heavyspoilers you want emails to watch  them make sure you check out our  breakdown of the perfect scene in  spider-man homecoming we point out  exactly why the moment in the car with  the vulture is absolutely outstanding  and it's definitely worth checking out  if you enjoyed this video if not then  thank you for sitting until the end of  this one i've been paul i'll see you  next time take care peace

